          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I can't breathe properly and my inhaler isn't working. My chest feels really tight.",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now.** This is an emergency. Do not wait to see if it passes.\n\nSit upright while you wait for help. If you have a reliever medicine in your own asthma action plan, follow that plan. Do not drive yourself. Tell the call handler that your breathing is severe and your inhaler is not helping. If someone is with you, ask them to stay with you.\n\nWhy this is an emergency: the guidance says severe breathing difficulty needs emergency care. This includes breathlessness that is not getting better after reliever treatment, and struggling to speak in full sentences. It also includes blue or grey lips or fingertips, drowsiness or confusion, and gasping or noisy breathing (Source: \"Severe difficulty breathing\", red-flag-severe-breathing-difficulty). Chest pain or tightness with breathlessness can also be a sign of a heart attack. That needs emergency care too (Source: \"Chest pain that may indicate a heart attack\", red-flag-chest-pain).\n\nDo not wait for these signs before calling. If you have any of them, call right away: lips or face turning blue or grey, confusion or unusual sleepiness, or being unable to speak in full sentences.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
